# 107. Canonical Project Progress Snapshot and Roadmap Visibility

TitanGold must provide the product owner with a simple, truthful and continuously
maintained view of project progress.

The purpose is to answer, without requiring the owner to read engineering logs:

- Where are we in the overall project?
- Which major milestone is active?
- Which stage of the current Program Slice is active?
- How many currently known stages remain?
- What is the next meaningful gate?
- Is progress blocked?

This mechanism is called the **TitanGold Progress Snapshot**.

The Progress Snapshot is operational visibility only.

It does not grant implementation authorization, production authorization, merge
authorization, Tier-4 approval or permission to skip any gate in this Rule.

Rule 02 remains the only Dynamic Active-Work implementation authority.

## 107.1 One Canonical Snapshot

There must be one canonical current Progress Snapshot inside Rule 02.

Do not create parallel progress counters in:

- Chat transcripts
- Cursor notes
- Frontend-only state
- Separate temporary status files
- PR descriptions
- Ad hoc spreadsheets

A living engineering document or Evidence Ledger may contain the detailed stage
breakdown, but Rule 02 must contain the short canonical summary.

The snapshot should normally contain only:

```text
PROJECT_PROGRESS = <completed_major_milestones>/<current_known_major_milestones>
CURRENT_MAJOR_MILESTONE = <name>
CURRENT_PROGRAM = <active Program Slice or outcome>
CURRENT_STAGE = <stage name>
CURRENT_STAGE_PROGRESS = <current_stage_index>/<current_known_stage_total>
REMAINING_STAGES = <count>
NEXT_GATE = <next meaningful gate>
PROGRESS_BLOCKER = NONE | <current blocker>
```

Additional progress fields may be added only when they materially improve owner
visibility.

Do not turn Rule 02 into a project-management database.

## 107.2 Counting Rules

Progress counts must represent meaningful outcomes or gates.

Do not inflate stage counts with:

- Individual commits
- Individual files
- One test rerun
- One small bug fix
- One documentation edit
- One diagnostic command
- Internal implementation details that do not represent a real transition

A stage should normally represent a meaningful state transition such as:

- Discovery complete
- Foundation complete
- Implementation complete
- Verification complete
- Human QA complete
- Production closeout complete
- A roadmap stage completed

The denominator is **the current known roadmap**, not a historical promise.

If discovery proves that stages must be added, removed, merged or split:

- Update the denominator truthfully
- Update `REMAINING_STAGES`
- Record the reason in the active Evidence Ledger or closeout evidence
- Do not preserve an obsolete count merely to make progress appear better

A blocked current stage remains the current stage.

Parallel internal workstreams do not become separate counted stages unless each
has an independent completion gate.

When this Core Rule already defines a canonical maturity or stage count, the
Progress Snapshot must use that canonical roadmap rather than invent another
count.

Examples include:

- Artemis Core Completion stages
- Automated-Trading maturity stages
- Autonomous Operations maturity stages
- Conversational maturity stages

## 107.3 Percentages and Time Estimates

Do not invent project percentages from unweighted stage counts.

Prefer:

`8/11 stages complete`

over:

`73% complete`

unless a canonical weighted-progress model exists.

Do not provide a time-to-completion estimate merely from stage count.

An ETA may be reported only when supported by an explicit planning estimate,
measured throughput or another defensible scheduling basis.

## 107.4 Mandatory Update Triggers

The Progress Snapshot must be updated when any of the following changes:

- A counted stage completes
- A stage is added, removed, merged or split
- The active Program Slice changes
- The active major milestone changes
- The next gate changes
- A blocker appears or clears
- A terminal STOP, FAILED, ROLLED_BACK or equivalent state changes the roadmap
- A new RCA introduces a required intermediate stage
- A roadmap stage is formally skipped as NOT APPLICABLE with evidence

Cursor must update the Progress Snapshot as part of the same legitimate Rule 02
or closeout update that records the state transition.

Do not create a standalone pointer, stamp or progress-only commit merely to
increment a counter.

For high-risk terminal closeout where Rule 02 must already change, the Progress
Snapshot must be corrected in that same closeout.

## 107.5 Cursor and Integrator Reporting

Every meaningful Cursor closeout report should include one compact progress line:

```text
PROGRESS: Project <X/Y> | Milestone <name> | Stage <A/B> | Remaining <N> | Next <gate> | Blocker <NONE/reason>
```

The Integrator reviewing Cursor output must verify that the progress line agrees
with Rule 02 and the actual engineering state.

If the Progress Snapshot is stale, contradictory or unsupported:

- Do not guess a replacement count
- Perform read-only reconciliation
- Correct the canonical snapshot before relying on it

When the owner asks questions such as:

- `How much is left?`
- `Which stage are we on?`
- `How many stages until Artemis?`
- `What comes next?`

the answer should be derived from the canonical Progress Snapshot and the
applicable roadmap, not from memory or informal chat history.

## 107.6 Owner-Facing Simplicity

The owner-facing status should remain short.

The normal human-readable form is:

```text
Project: <X/Y major milestones>
Current: <program> — Stage <A/B>
Remaining in this program: <N>
Next: <gate>
Blocked: <No / short reason>
```

Detailed engineering evidence remains available separately when needed.

The Progress Snapshot must reduce coordination burden, not create another
approval layer.

## 107.7 Initial Bootstrap and Rebaseline

For an existing project that did not previously maintain this snapshot:

- Do not fabricate historical counts
- Initialize the first canonical snapshot from the verified current roadmap
- Use the next legitimate Rule 02 closeout or explicit roadmap rebaseline
- Record only currently known major milestones and stages

When a major roadmap is materially revised, perform one concise rebaseline and
continue from the new truthful denominator.

Past completed work remains completed unless a separate authorized defect or
reopening rule requires otherwise.

## 107.8 Final Progress-Visibility Principle

The goal is not to make the roadmap look fixed.

The goal is:

> The owner should always be able to see where TitanGold is, what remains, what
> comes next and what is blocking progress, using one short canonical status that
> stays truthful as the engineering plan evolves.

